<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  GeneralsX @build Android R8 protection 14/11/2026

  Resource shrinker guard for the launcher's palette lookup.

  ThemeHelper resolves a screen's colours at runtime by BUILDING the resource
  name and asking the platform for it:

      res.getIdentifier("gzh_navy_" + role, "color", packageName)
      res.getIdentifier("gzh_accent_" + accent + "_" + role, "color", packageName)
      res.getIdentifier("gzh_day_accent_" + accent + "_primary", "color", packageName)

  The shrinker only sees resources the code reaches through a generated R.*
  reference, so a colour used ONLY this way looks unused and would be deleted.
  The app would then silently fall back to the wrong colour (getIdentifier
  returns 0 and ThemeHelper falls through to the base palette) instead of
  crashing, which is exactly the kind of failure a build has no way to catch.

  Every name this lookup can produce starts with "gzh_", so keeping that prefix
  keeps all of them: the base palette (values/colors.xml), the navy and day
  palettes (values/colors_palettes.xml) and the accent colours
  (values/colors_accents.xml, values/colors_accents_day.xml).

  The pattern is a prefix wildcard, not "@color/*": the launcher's own R.color
  references and the theme/drawable references keep everything else, and keeping
  every colour would blunt the shrinker for no reason.

  The colors that ARE reached through R.color.* (UiKit.color(), the theme XML)
  need no entry here: the shrinker sees those references itself.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    tools:keep="@color/gzh_*" />
